Otitis media
Opthalmology & ENT
Bulging tympanic membrane, Throbbing otalgia, Conductive hearing loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, inconsolable crying in a young child with high fever and ear tugging.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a highly irritable, febrile toddler exhibiting severe unilateral ear pain that worsens immediately upon lying down. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute mastoiditis, meningitis, or impending tympanic membrane rupture, and to provide immediate, weight-based pharmacological analgesia to relieve the severe distress preventing sleep for the entire household.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He has been screaming inconsolably for hours, his fever is spiking, and he keeps violently pulling at his right ear every time we lie him down."
History of Present Illness: 2-year-old patient presents with a 12-hour history of acute, severe, throbbing right ear pain (otalgia) that significantly worsens when in a supine position. Parents report high systemic fever and generalized marked irritability. Patient exhibits vigorous tugging, pulling, and rubbing of the right ear. Parents note a subjective decrease in his responsiveness to soft sounds on that side. Distinct antecedent symptoms of a viral upper respiratory tract infection (clear rhinorrhea, mild cough) for the past 3 days. Denies vomiting, neck stiffness, or rash.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, crying continuously, unable to settle or sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: Exhausted, highly anxious parents.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 130 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 26 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 14 Kg
General: Febrile, flushed, clinging to mother, actively crying but consolable temporarily. No toxic appearance, no photophobia.
Physical Exam: Otoscopy of the left ear reveals a mildly erythematous but non-bulging tympanic membrane. Otoscopy of the right ear reveals a severely bulging, intensely erythematous, and completely opaque tympanic membrane distinctly lacking a normal light reflex. The landmarks (malleus) are completely obscured by the middle ear effusion. Decreased tympanic membrane mobility noted. Neck is supple; no nuchal rigidity. No post-auricular swelling, erythema, or tenderness over the mastoid process. Chest is clear.
Environmental Observations: Multiple used tissues and half-empty bottles of infant paracetamol present.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Parents under-dosing oral Paracetamol due to fear of toxicity.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Suppurative Otitis Media - Status: Acute, Severe
Differentials:

Mastoiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal external post-auricular examination with no forward protrusion of the pinna, and the ear pain is localized to the middle ear, not the mastoid bone.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The child being alert, active (though distressed), consolable, with a supple neck, no petechial rash, and a clear localized source of the high fever.

Otitis Externa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of pain on tragal pressure or pinna traction, and the external auditory canal is clear, allowing direct visualization of the diseased tympanic membrane.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient presented with classic symptoms of an acute middle ear infection driving severe pain and high fever. Immediate clinical evaluation was essential to rule out dangerous central or contiguous spread (meningitis, mastoiditis). The physical examination definitively localized the infection behind a severely bulging tympanic membrane, explaining the intense pain exacerbation when supine (due to increased venous pressure). By correcting the parental under-dosing of analgesics and confirming the diagnosis of a severe bacterial AOM (indicated by the severe bulging and high fever), the urgent visit prevented an emergency department presentation and rapidly restored comfort to the child.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Amoxicillin 50mg/kg/day in divided doses for 5 days due to the severity of the bulging and the young age. Corrected weight-based dosing for oral Paracetamol and Ibuprofen, advising alternating doses for maximum analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure accurate and safe medication dosing, the parents must physically draw up the exact required volume of liquid analgesic into a syringe, mark the line with a permanent marker, and strictly use only that syringe for future doses. This physical fail-safe removes the cognitive burden of late-night math, instantly providing confidence and preventing under-dosing (positive reinforcement through effective pain relief).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours to ensure clinical improvement and eventual resolution of the middle ear effusion.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child develops a stiff neck, becomes unusually drowsy or difficult to wake, develops a new purple/red rash that does not fade when pressed, or if the redness spreads behind the ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a middle ear bacterial infection; fluid may remain for weeks after the infection clears.
Clinical Course: The fever and severe pain should resolve within 48 hours of starting the antibiotics and correct pain relief. A sudden discharge of fluid from the ear means the eardrum has burst, which relieves pain and heals well, but requires a GP check.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your child becomes floppy, extremely difficult to wake up, or develops a rash that looks like tiny bruises, you must call 000 immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria from a recent cold have traveled up the tube into the middle ear, causing pus to build up and stretch the eardrum. This stretching causes agonizing pain, especially when lying flat. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria.
Actionable Steps: Give the correct, weight-based doses of pain relief strictly by the clock, do not wait for the child to start crying again. Elevate the head of the cot slightly to reduce pressure on the eardrum tonight.

